<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-06-sensitivity1">
    <title>06 - Sensitivity Analysis Part 1</title>

    <!-- Optional introduction -->
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In this activity we want to analyze how sensitive a model is to its
            parameters.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <!-- Exercises start here. -->
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                The population of Zika mosquitoes can be modeled with the
                function
                <md>
                    P(t) = 8000\left(\frac{1}{6} + e^{-\frac{(t-c)^2}{d}} + e^{-\frac{(t-9)^2}{6}}\right)
                </md>
                where <m>t</m> is given in months.
                We will investigate the effect of the parameters <m>c</m> and
                <m>d</m> on the model.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Create a Google Colab document that plots the model for
                    <m>{c = 3}</m> and <m>{d = 6}</m>.
                    Let <m>t</m> range from 0 to 12.
                    Define the function for your model to take <m>c</m> and
                    <m>d</m> as inputs, along with the variable <m>t</m>.
                    You might have this from the last worksheet!
                    Make sure to import the following packages:
                    <ul>
                        <li><c>import math</c></li>
                        <li><c>import numpy as np</c></li>
                        <li><c>import matplotlib.pyplot as plt</c></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let's start by seeing what happens when we change the
                    parameter <m>c</m> drastically.
                    Let <m>d = 6</m> as before.
                    Plot the population of mosquitos for
                    <m>{c = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}</m>.
                    Let's do this using a for loop.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Begin by creating a list of the <m>c</m> values using the
                    following command: <c>clist = np.arange(1,7,1)</c>.
                    This creates "clist" from 1 (inclusive) to 7 (exclusive)
                    with steps of size 1.
                </p>

                <p>
                    To create a legend for your plot that differentiates between
                    the <m>c</m> values, add a label to your plot line of code:
                    <c>plt.plot(t,y, label='c='+str(c))</c>.
                </p>

                <p>
                    What do you notice?
                    How does the model of the population change?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let's look at what happens if rather than drastic changes in
                    <m>c</m>, we only change <m>c</m> by about <m>2\%</m>.
                    To do this, let's look at the outputs for
                    <m>{c = 2.88, 2.94,
                    3, 3.06, 3.12}</m>.
                    Let's keep track of the relative minimum in between the two
                    "humps" of the curve and compare how the output changes
                    based on how the input changed.
                    If the parameter <m>c</m> changes by 0.06 (about <m>2\%</m>
                    ), how much does the relative minimum of the population
                    change?
                </p>

                <p>
                    Some helpful things to consider:
                    <ul>
                        <li>numpy has the function <c>np.min()</c></li>
                        <li>
                            to find the percent change in the output at the
                            relative min, we take the difference in the current
                            output and the previous output values, divide by the
                            previous output, and turn it into a percentage
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Based on this analysis, do you think the model is sensitive
                    to changes in the parameter <m>c</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Now let's look at the affect of changing the parameter
                    <m>d</m>.
                    Let's begin by changing the parameter <m>d</m> drastically.
                    Let <m>c = 3</m> as we did at the beginning.
                    Plot the population of mosquitos for
                    <m>{d = 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9}</m>.
                </p>

                <p>
                    What do you notice?
                    How does the model of the population change?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let's look at what happens if rather than drastic changes in
                    <m>d</m>, we only change <m>d</m> by about <m>2\%</m>.
                    To do this, let's look at the outputs for
                    <m>{d = 5.76, 5.88, 6, 6.12, 6.24}</m>.
                    Let's again keep track of the relative minimum in between
                    the two "humps" of the curve and compare how the output
                    changes based on how the input changed.
                    If the parameter <m>d</m> changes by 0.12 (about <m>2\%</m>
                    ), how much does the relative minimum of the population
                    change?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Based on this analysis, do you think the model is sensitive
                    to changes in the parameter <m>d</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Is the model more sensitive to changes in the parameter
                    <m>c</m> or <m>d</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
    <introduction>
    <p>
        Assume we introduce a new species to an area and monitor
        the population over 24 years. Let <m>t</m> be the number of 
        years after the introduction of the new species, and <m>f(t)</m>
        be the number of animals in the area at time <m>t</m>. Assume
        also that <m>f(t)</m> is modeled fairly well by
        <md> f(t) = -\frac{1}{4}(at)^3 + 6t^2 + 10t</md>
        where <m>a</m> is an unknown parameter. However, when we fit the
        model to data we find that <m>a</m> is about 1. We want to 
        determine if our model is sensitive to the parameter <m>a</m>.
        </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                   Plot the model with <m>a = 1</m> and show the first
                   24 years. Then plot the model for <m>{a = 0.99}</m>
                   and <m>{a = 1.01}</m>. Put all three plots on the 
                   same graph. Notice that there is a <m>1\%</m> change in
                   the parameter <m>a</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                   Determine the percent change in the output of the 
                   model at the end of the time period, 24 years. 
                   That is, when <m>a</m> is changed by <m>1\%</m>, 
                   how much does the population change by at the end 
                   of 24 years?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                   In your opinion, is this model sensitive to the parameter
                   <m>a</m>? Why or why not?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>